{"name":"Honeydew Melon","hex":"#e6eccc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honeydew melon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A melon from the Cucumis melo Inodorus cultivar group, with sweet, light green or white flesh and a smooth greenish-white or yellow rind."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honeydew_melon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Honeydew (melon)","blocks":[{"text":"The honeydew melon is one of the two main cultivar types in Cucumis melo Inodorus Group. It is characterized by the smooth, often green or yellowish rind and lack of musky odor. The other main type in the Inodorus Group is the wrinkle-rind casaba melon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Honeydew.jpg/330px-Honeydew.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeydew_(melon)","wikidata":"Q47801"},{"title":"Melon","blocks":[{"text":"A melon is any of various plants of the family Cucurbitaceae with sweet, edible, and fleshy fruit. It can also specifically refer to Cucumis melo, commonly known as the \"true melon\" or simply \"melon\". The term \"melon\" can apply to both the plant and its fruit. Botanically, a melon is a kind of berry, specifically a \"pepo\". The word melon derives from Latin melopepo, which is the latinization of the Greek μηλοπέπων (mēlopepōn), meaning \"melon\", itself a compound of μῆλον (mēlon), \"apple\", treefruit \" and πέπων (pepōn), amongst others \"a kind of gourd or melon\". Many different cultivars have been produced, particularly of the true melon, such as the cantaloupe and honeydew."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg/330px-Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melon","wikidata":"Q5881191"},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green","wikidata":"Q19847180"},{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange","wikidata":"Q19847194"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Honeydew_melon.jpg/500px-Honeydew_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Honeydew_melon.jpg/1280px-Honeydew_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Honeydew_melon.jpg/500px-Honeydew_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Honeydew melon, Kolkata, West Bengal, India","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHoneydew_melon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Honeyde